! mcp_bootstrap_p155.strap -- "WRITE LABEL", printed page 155.
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.162.  Header read and not
! assumed: the top line prints "TIME CLOCK 00054041  PUNID
! 11011MCP   CARD ID IB000088   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE
! NUMBER 155".
!
! >>> THE HEADING ROW'S OWN LOCATION FIELD READS 217704. <<<
! Rendered at 900 dpi and looked at, not guessed: the column-
! heading line prints "LINE  LOCATION  BINARY OUTPUT  NAME
! STATEMENT  LOCATION   217704", with four clean digits after the
! 2177.  That is LINE 1'S LOCATION WORD (docs/transcription_
! workflow.md step 1), and line 1 is 217704.40 -- the word agrees,
! and the field gives no bit offset, so the ".40" is this deck's
! own reading of the LOCATION column and not the header's.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Forty-eight printed lines.  Forty-four of them are located,
! emitting rows; one (line 14) is a CNOP that emits NOTHING; three
! (lines 8, 9, 10) are the comment card that boxes the heading
! "***************** WRITE LABEL *****************".  No SYN, no
! DD, no VF, no DR/DRZ -- no data of any kind -- so rule 5 of
! "WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE" (DR vs DRZ) does not arise here.
!
!   lines  1-7   the tail of the CHANGE DENSITY routine that
!                printed p.154 begins: turn on the set-up bit,
!                CNOP/LX/SX the instruction into S IO INS, issue
!                "K DEN OP  CTL,0.0($14),0.0  -CHANGE DENSITY
!                COMMAND", then LVI,1,K SUPP / B,M IO REJ to check
!                whether the channel accepted it.
!   lines  8-10  the asterisk box around "WRITE LABEL".
!   K W LABL (11) backspace the block: LVI,1,K SUPP, pick up the
!                current reel, branch to K ILEGL if the file is
!                protected, then the CNOP/LX/SX/CTL sequence that
!                issues the BSP command, SIC,S RET AD / B,M IO REJ
!                and SIC,K K IND / B,K IO IND.
!   lines 23-34  BUILD THE DENSITY/PARITY BCD CHARACTER.  Load the
!                file mode, branch to Z WLABL if it is zero, then
!                a four-way decision written as connectives:
!                CTI0110 tests for mode 11 (no-ECC odd), LFI plants
!                BCD 0, CI0111 ORs in BCD C or E, C0011 loads the
!                IOD density bit, and CI0111 again makes BCD L for
!                low density, or C1111 makes BCD H for high.
!   lines 35-41  fill in the label image: LVE/SV plant the label
!                control word E LAB CW, then four SF (= CM0101,
!                Store Field) stores lay the mode, the density and
!                a 24-bit blank field (8)202020.20 into S SCR PM.
!   lines 42-48  CNOP, LX/SX the instruction into S IO INS, issue
!                "W,0.0($14),E LAB CW  -WRITE LABEL COMMAND",
!                LVI,1,K SUPP, clear S CTL and set S SET UP again.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FORTY-ONE CARDS OVER FORTY-EIGHT LINES; NOTHING FELL OFF EITHER
! END
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! COUNTED OFF THE CARD-SERIAL COLUMN, not off the survey.  The
! serials run IB000088 (line 1) to IB000128 (line 48) with no gap,
! and SEVEN serials are printed TWICE, on two statements each:
!
!   IB000090  lines  3 and  4   LX,0,$+1.   + SX,0,S IOINS($12)
!   IB000092  lines  6 and  7   LVI,1,K SUPP + B,M IO REJ
!   IB000101  lines 16 and 17   LX,0,$+1.   + SX,0,S IO INS($12)
!   IB000103  lines 19 and 20   SIC,S RET AD($12) + B,M IO REJ
!   IB000104  lines 21 and 22   SIC,K K IND + B,K IO IND
!   IB000117  lines 35 and 36   LVE,0,R V4  + SV,0,E LAB CW
!   IB000124  lines 43 and 44   LX,0,$+1.0  + SX,0,S IO INS($12)
!
! 41 + 7 = 48, so every printed line is accounted for and the
! render lost nothing.  Line 1 is the page's first line (the
! render starts at y=480 at 400 dpi, which is LINE 1) and line 48
! is followed by blank paper to the bottom rule.
!
! NOTE which pairs share a card and which do not: the two SIC/
! branch pairs on 19/20 and 21/22 DO share, as does the LVI/B pair
! on 6/7 -- but the LX/SX pairs share as well, and lines 45/46
! (W + LVI) do NOT.  "A SIC and its branch share a card" is not
! what is happening; what shares a card here is simply a card with
! two statements punched on it, and the kinds vary.
!
! >>> WHERE THIS DECK DISAGREES WITH THE SURVEY. <<<  The survey
! reported 25 rows spanning 217705.40..217737.4.  This page has 44
! located statements emitting 64 HALF WORDS and runs
! 217704.40..217743.40.  (64 counted twice: 20 two-half-word rows
! plus 24 one-half-word rows is 64, and 0o217744.40 - 0o217704.40
! is 0o40 words = 32 words = 64 half words, which agree because
! the CNOP chain below shows the span is gapless.)  The survey lost LINE 1 off the top (rule
! 4's predicted failure, exactly) and everything from line 42 on
! -- the CNOP/LX/SX/W that actually issue the write -- and it
! under-read the middle.  This deck wins because all 66 half words
! are checked against the printed BINARY OUTPUT column below and
! every one agrees, and because the card-serial arithmetic above
! closes independently of the binary.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE CNOP PARITY CHAIN -- A FREE WHOLE-PAGE CHECK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This page prints THREE CNOPs and one of them emits NOTHING.
! That is not an anomaly, it is the alignment rule, and because a
! CNOP's output depends on the counter's parity it turns into an
! assertion about every row above it:
!
!   line  2  at 217705.40, odd half word  -> emits 000000.30 00
!   line 14  no location, no binary       -> emits NOTHING,
!            because line 13's two half words leave the counter at
!            217713.00, a word boundary
!   line 42  at 217737.40, odd half word  -> emits 000000.30 00
!
! So if ANY width in lines 1-13 were wrong, line 14's CNOP would
! emit a half word and every location from 217713.00 down would
! shift by .40 -- and forty rows of !CHECK would fail at once.
! Line 14 therefore appears in the deck as a statement with no
! !CHECK row of its own, and it is the strongest single structural
! claim on the page.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOIN
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! NEAR SIDE.  This page prints NO SLC, so the counter arrives from
! printed p.154 and this deck restates it hard as
! "SLC,(8)217704.40" in order to assemble standalone.  A hard SLC
! CANNOT DISAGREE WITH THE ARRIVING COUNTER (docs/transcription_
! workflow.md), so that line ASSERTS the origin; what READS it is
! the heading row's own LOCATION field, 217704, printed by IBM's
! assembler and rendered at 900 dpi here.  The bit offset .40 is
! read off line 1's LOCATION column.
!
! >>> A FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION ABOUT PRINTED p.154. <<<  Line 1 is
! at 217704.40, the ODD half word of word 217704.  So p.154's last
! emitting row occupied 217704.00 and was a HALF WORD; p.154
! CANNOT end on a word boundary, and it cannot end at 217704.40
! either.  If p.154's transcriber reports a last row at 217703.40
! or at 217704.40, one of us is wrong.
!
! >>> AND IT IS ALREADY CONFIRMED, off printed p.154's own IMAGE
! (PDF p.161) rendered at 1200 dpi -- not off an OCR.  p.154's
! line 48 is
!
!   48*  217704.00   217707.43 D0        SVA,1,K DEN OP+.32
!
! -- a HALF WORD at 217704.00, exactly as predicted, and it is a
! forward reference INTO THIS PAGE: K DEN OP + 32 bits is
! 217707.40, so that row pins THIS page's line 5 label to
! 217707.00 from outside, and prints the name K DEN OP in its own
! ink with the D's straight left stem plainly visible.
!
! FAR SIDE.  Line 48 is a two-half-word VFL at 217743.40, so it
! occupies 217743.40 and 217744.00 and THE COUNTER LEAVES THIS
! PAGE AT 217744.40 -- an odd half word.  Printed p.156 opens
! there unless it prints an SLC of its own.
!
! >>> AND THAT FORWARD JOIN IS ALREADY CLOSED, by the volume OCR
! OF ANOTHER PAGE -- which is the one thing that OCR is good for
! (docs/transcription_workflow.md: "an excellent index of where a
! symbol is DEFINED on a page nobody has opened yet").
! docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_001-249.txt's block for PDF page 163 =
! printed p.156 opens
!
!   LINE LOCATICN ...
!   1 217744.40 05.C C SIC,S RET AC( $12)
!
! -- line 1 at 217744.40, which is this page's predicted exit
! EXACTLY, and the row is a half-word SIC.  (The prediction is
! still recorded above as a prediction: it was made from this
! page's own counter before that block was read, and p.156's
! transcriber should re-read it off the image, not off the OCR.)
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPHS, AND HOW EACH DOUBTFUL ONE WAS SETTLED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIS PAGE DROPS INK ON ROUND GLYPHS CONSTANTLY.  Line 5's first
! half word prints "CCCCCC.CC 8E" and line 18's prints
! "CCCCCC.CC 8E" -- six and six impossible C's in OCTAL fields --
! so the octal-field argument (ladder rung 5) fires on this page
! harder than on any page yet transcribed.  Every "C" recorded
! below as a 0 is in a field that cannot hold a C.
!
! (1) THE CONNECTIVE MNEMONICS, at 900 dpi, are the one place the
!     reading was genuinely in doubt, because an O/0 confusion
!     inside "C0110" changes an instruction:
!       line 25  CTI0110   C T I 0 1 1 0  -- seven characters
!       line 29  CI0111    C I 0 1 1 1    -- six
!       line 30  C0011     C 0 0 1 1      -- five
!       line 32  C1111     C 1 1 1 1      -- five
!       line 34  CI0111    C I 0 1 1 1    -- six
!     What makes this safe is that classify_connective takes
!     C/CM/CT plus EXACTLY four binary digits, and the I-infix
!     immediate form is rewritten to the suffix form -- so a
!     miscount of the digits is not an instruction at all and the
!     assembler refuses it.  All five were handed to it in the
!     printed form and all five assembled; the bits then agree
!     with the printed BINARY OUTPUT column, which is what pins
!     the digits themselves.
!
! (2) "K DEN OP" (line 5) -- settled three ways.  The comment
!     column is the oracle: "-CHANGE DENSITY COMMAND" gives DEN,
!     and this page's own line 30 reads S F DEN against p.102's
!     "-IOD DENSITY" card.  The "... OP" suffix has precedent in
!     p.101's "S CTL OP".  AND PRINTED p.154's LINE 48 PRINTS THE
!     NAME ITSELF, on another page and in a different row of ink:
!     "217704.00  217707.43 D0  SVA,1,K DEN OP+.32", rendered at
!     1200 dpi, D's straight left stem and all -- and its
!     arithmetic lands on 217707.40, this page's line 5 plus 32
!     bits, so the name and the address come together.
!
! (3) "K K IND" (line 21) and "K IO IND" (line 22) are read off
!     the ink.  The doubled-prefix shape is this module's: p.152
!     prints "K K MODE" beside "K MODE" at 217624.40/217615.00,
!     and these two sit one half word apart at 221371.00 and
!     221370.00, the same SIC-here/branch-there shape.
!
! (4) "Z WLABL" (line 27, and referenced by line 24) prints with a
!     blank after the Z and none inside WLABL; "K W LABL" (line
!     11) prints with blanks on both sides of the W.  BOTH close
!     up to six characters, and they are DIFFERENT SYMBOLS at
!     different addresses (217723.00 and 217711.00) -- the page
!     prints them four lines apart and the branch on line 24 goes
!     to the Z one, which is what the bits say.
!
! (5) THE BYTES THAT LOOKED DOUBTFUL WERE SETTLED ON WHAT PRODUCED
!     THEM, not on ink:
!     * lines 1, 15 and 48 all print "001000.36 F0"; F0 is CM, and
!       printed p.18 prints CM1111(BU,1) six times, every one
!       "001000.36 F0".
!     * line 47's second word prints "001000.CC FC" -- the ".CC"
!       is an impossible octal C, so that row IS dropping ink on
!       round glyphs, and the byte's open second glyph is a
!       damaged 0.  The statement settles it outright: CM0000 with
!       no offset operand has i2 = 0, so the byte ends in 0.
!     * lines 26 and 31 print "C0" where lines 24 prints "C2".
!       Those are BZRZ and BRZ and the assembler reproduces both
!       exactly as printed.
!
! (6) THE COMMENT COLUMN, recorded verbatim including its own
!     typos -- line 31's is printed "DESITY", not "DENSITY", and
!     line 27's is "NECC ODD" where line 25's is "NO ECC ODD".
!     Neither is corrected here; they are what the card punched.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> "EU" AND "EF": A SYMBOLIC INDEX, AND ONLY HALF OF IT IS READ
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Two statements write their index register as a NAME instead of
! "$n":
!
!   line 48   CM1111,S SET UP(EU)        -> 000000.37 8B
!   line 23   L,S F MODE(EF),1           -> 000002.60 8D
!
! EU == $11 IS PINNED BY THIS PAGE ITSELF, by glyph-ladder rung 4
! (the same statement printed twice on one page).  Lines 1 and 15
! print "CM1111(BU,1),S SET UP($11)" and compile to
! "000000.37 8B  001000.36 F0"; line 48 prints
! "CM1111,S SET UP(EU)" and compiles to THE SAME TWO HALF WORDS.
! Same page, same drum, same symbol, and the low nibble of the
! first byte is B = 11.  That also shows the second thing line 48
! is doing: with no "(BU,1)" written, the data description comes
! from S SET UP's own SYN, and the word is unchanged.
!
! EF == $13 is read the same way: the printed low nibble is D =
! 13, and printed p.151 line 38 prints "LV,1,S F MODE($13)" for
! the same symbol.
!
! >>> AND THE DEFINING CARDS ARE ON PRINTED p.154, READ OFF ITS
! OWN IMAGE AT 900 dpi. <<<  p.154's lines 23-28 are a block of
! index equivalences, printed as NULL cards:
!
!   000014.00+  +00000000  NULL  EA  SYN,12.0  -UNIT AREA TAB[LE]
!                       -               $10    -CHANNEL STATUS TAB
!   000015.00+  +00000000  NULL  EF  SYN,13.0  -FILE AREA TAB[LE]
!   000010.00+  +00000000  NULL  EX  SYN,8.0   -SCRATCH INDEX
!                       -               $9      SCRATCH INDEX
!   000013.00+  +00000000  NULL  EU  SYN,11.0  -UNIT STATUS T[ABLE]
!
! So the SPELLINGS ARE SETTLED, not guessed: EA=12, EF=13, EX=8,
! EU=11, each with an English comment naming the table it indexes,
! and each with its own value printed in the BINARY OUTPUT column
! in octal (000014 = 12, 000015 = 13, 000010 = 8, 000013 = 11).
! NOTE that the volume OCR reads that last card "ER", which is
! WRONG -- the image prints a U with a rounded bottom -- and that
! printed p.154's line 21 independently prints "CM1111,S WR(EU)
! -TURN ON WRITE BIT IN U.S.T.", the Unit Status Table, which is
! what the SYN's own comment calls it.  That is why the OCR is an
! index and not a witness.
!
! >>> A HAZARD FOR THE MERGE, MEASURED. <<<  The printed defining
! form is "SYN,11.0", a DOTTED literal, and in this assembler a
! symbol so defined is a BIT COUNT -- 11*64 -- while an index
! field takes eval_expr(...) & 0xF.  Measured, varying only the
! SYN and nothing else, against this page's line 48:
!
!   EU  SYN,11      ->  000000.37 8B  001000.36 F0   (the page)
!   EU  SYN,$11     ->  000000.37 8B  001000.36 F0   (the page)
!   EU  SYN,11.0    ->  000000.37 80  001000.36 F0   <-- index LOST
!   EU  SYN,(8)13.0 ->  000000.37 80  001000.36 F0   <-- index LOST
!
! So if printed p.154's deck writes its index equivalences in the
! PRINTED form "SYN,11.0", every instruction on THIS page and on
! p.154 that indexes through one of them will silently compile
! with index 0.  THIS DECK THEREFORE WRITES "SYN, 11" AND "SYN,
! 13", labelled here as a deliberate departure from the printed
! form, with the printed form recorded above.  It is the same
! root cause as finding (A) below: eval_expr returns a bit count
! where the field wants the word number.
!
! >>> AND THE MANUAL SAYS THE PRINTED FORM IS THE RIGHT ONE. <<<
! C22-6531 "Arithmetic Expressions", printed p.11, lists five
! restrictions on what may appear in a symbolized field, and the
! FIRST is: "The 'I' or 'K' index field must contain at least one
! bit address term."  "SYN,11.0" IS a bit-address term; "SYN,11"
! is an integer and is the form the manual excludes.  So p.154's
! cards are correct STRAP and this assembler is the thing that
! does not take them, which makes this a GAP and not a quirk of
! the listing.  The same manual's worked example fixes the
! conversion: "$5 ... by the index field 5 if it appears in an I
! or J field, or by the address 21.0 if it appears in an address
! field" -- so in an index field a term is reduced to an index
! NUMBER.  A candidate fix, NOT applied here and offered with its
! own refutation test: give eval_reg the eval_addr_field
! treatment at unit_shift 6 (bit-address terms >> 6, integers
! as-is, "$N" still N in reg_context).  IT MUST NOT BREAK "($12)"
! -- if it does, it is wrong.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> WHAT THIS DECK COSTS THE MERGED IMAGE, STATED PLAINLY <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! As it stands, joining this page to the image DEPOSITS TWO WRONG
! WORDS: 017600.05 00 at 217715.00 where the page prints
! 000176.05 00, and 003000.36 70 at 217727.00 where the page
! prints 003004.76 70.  No check inside this deck hides that --
! both rows FAIL by design -- but nothing in strap/ or
! strap/mcp_image.py may be touched from here, so the decision is
! the caller's.  RECOMMENDATION: do not add printed p.155 to
! MCP_IPL_PAGES until finding (A) and finding (B) are fixed; the
! deck is correct as a transcription either way.  Finding (A)'s
! candidate fix is one line and already has a passing test --
! "(8)176." -> 126, "(8)176" -> 126 and io_tape.strap's bare 126
! -> 126 all hold under eval_addr_field at unit_shift 6.
!
! >>> AND ONE MERGE COLLISION TO EXPECT, WHICH IS LOUD <<<
! This deck defines EU and EF, and so will printed p.154's, whose
! page carries the actual cards.  Measured on the two paths:
!
!   strap_asm.assemble() of the two definitions concatenated --
!     SILENT.  The LAST definition wins; "EU SYN,11.0" then
!     "EU SYN,11" gives byte 8B, the reverse order gives byte 80,
!     with no diagnostic either way.
!   mcp_image.merge() -- LOUD.  Neither form matches _STANDIN's
!     "(8)NNN.NN" pattern, so both land in `defines`, and two
!     disagreeing `defines` are a hard SystemExit naming both
!     pages and both bit values (11 against 704).
!
! So the image build CANNOT silently take the wrong one.  What it
! WILL do is refuse to build until p.154 and p.155 agree, and the
! right resolution is the integer form -- see the EU/EF section.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT COULD NOT BE SETTLED ON THIS PAGE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (i)  THE BIT OFFSETS OF THE SIX CODE EXTERNALS.  K SUPP,
!      M IO REJ, K ILEGL, K IO IND, R V4 and E LAB CW are all
!      back-derived from branches and LVI/LVE/SV/W instructions,
!      which show only the half-word bit.  Each is written ".0"
!      by convention, NOT by reading.  K K IND is the exception
!      and is pinned to the bit, because line 21's SIC prints a
!      bare 24-bit address.
!
! (ii) WHETHER STRAP'S RULE FOR A DOTTED LITERAL IN A CTL CODE
!      FIELD IS WHAT FINDING (A) INFERS.  Two listings agree
!      (this page's line 18 and printed p.156's line 14) and the
!      value is corroborated by decks/io_tape.strap, but NO
!      MANUAL SENTENCE was found that states the CONTROL field's
!      units.  C22-6531 p.11's five restrictions cover the index
!      field and the parenthetical-integer field and say nothing
!      about this one.  So (A) is two witnesses and an inference,
!      not a citation.
!
! (iii) WHAT STRAP MEANT BY THE EMPTY ADDRESS FIELD IN LINE 32'S
!      "C1111(BU,3),,9".  The bits establish that the ADDRESS
!      came out zero and the OFFSET survived as 9; they do not
!      establish whether an omitted field is defined to be zero
!      or whether the card is relying on something else.  Nothing
!      on this page distinguishes those.
!
! (iv) THE ONE-SPACE INDENT ON LINE 36.  "SV,0,E LAB CW" starts
!      one character to the right of every other statement on the
!      page, and it is the second statement on card IB000117.
!      Recorded because it is printed; nothing depends on it, and
!      no other shared card on this page is indented.
!
! (v)  THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK, as always.  See below.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every line number carries an asterisk after it ("5*", "48*").
! That is the ordinary column.  THREE lines carry a SECOND
! asterisk after the LOCATION: line 2 (217705.40, a CNOP), line 24
! (217721.00, a BRZ) and line 39 (217734.40, an SF).  Recorded and
! nothing proposed: a CNOP, a branch and a store-field have
! nothing in common, p.152's marker fell on an SX, and the two
! rules tried so far ("the SLC line is marked", "an odd half word
! is marked") are both already dead -- line 2 and line 39 ARE odd
! half words but so are thirty other rows here, none marked.
! docs/listing_typography.md item 12 now has NINE things it is
! not.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> TWO ROWS ARE LEFT FAILING ON PURPOSE. <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Both printed statements were handed to strap_asm EXACTLY AS
! PRINTED, both assembled without complaint, and both produced a
! word that is NOT the word the page prints.  Neither affects the
! location counter -- each is a two-half-word VFL/I-O either way,
! so every row below them still lands on its printed address --
! so nothing had to be substituted to keep the page honest, and
! per docs/transcription_workflow.md and the block brief a FAILING
! row beats a silent substitution.  Each is measured to the token.
!
! (A) LINE 18, "CTL,0.0($14),(8)176."   -BSP COMMAND
!     printed   000000.00 8E  000176.05 00
!     assembled 000000.00 8E  017600.05 00     <-- 64x too big
!
!     The trailing "." is really printed: rendered at 1200 dpi,
!     "(8)176." with a period and nothing after it.  Measured,
!     varying only that token and nothing else:
!
!       CTL,0.0($14),(8)176.   ->  017600.05 00
!       CTL,0.0($14),(8)176    ->  000176.05 00   (the printed word)
!       CTL,0.0($14),126       ->  000176.05 00   (the printed word)
!       CTL,0.0($14),176       ->  000260.05 00
!
!     WHY: emit_statement's I-O branch takes CTL/LOC's second field
!     as "a raw code, not an address of anything", so it evaluates
!     it with eval_expr -- which returns a BIT COUNT -- and masks
!     it in unconverted.  A dotted literal "(8)176." is therefore
!     126*64 = 8064, while STRAP evidently took its WORD number,
!     176 octal.  Note that line 5 ("CTL,0.0($14),0.0") and line 45
!     ("W,0.0($14),E LAB CW") CANNOT see this: line 5's operand is
!     zero, and line 45 is a W, whose A field IS converted (>>6).
!     THIS ROW IS THE FIRST WITNESS IN THE CORPUS of a CTL code
!     field written as a dotted literal.
!
!     The VALUE is corroborated from outside this page:
!     decks/io_tape.strap writes the same tape function as
!     "CTL, 0($1), 126   !BACKSPACE BLOCK (octal 176)", which is
!     what line 18's own comment "-BSP COMMAND" says it is.
!
!     AND THE FORM HAS A SECOND WITNESS ON ANOTHER PRINTED PAGE.
!     The volume OCR's block for PDF page 163 = printed p.156
!     carries, at its line 14,
!
!       14# 217753.CC0 O00C0. 8E O000117.C5 OC
!                             CTL,0.0($14),(8) 117.  -WEF CCMMAND
!
!     i.e. the same dotted-literal spelling "(8)117." printing
!     "000117.05 00" -- word number into the field, again.  So
!     this is a RULE about STRAP's CTL code field and not a
!     one-card oddity, and printed p.156 will hit the identical
!     failure.  (That is an OCR of a DIFFERENT page, so it is a
!     legitimate index; p.156's transcriber must re-read it off
!     the image.)
!
!     A candidate one-line fix, NOT applied here (this deck may
!     not touch strap/): evaluate the CTL/LOC code field with
!     eval_addr_field(..., unit_shift=6), which converts a
!     bit-address term to a word count and still inserts a bare
!     integer as itself -- "(8)176." and "(8)176" would then BOTH
!     render 000176 and io_tape.strap's "126" would be unchanged.
!     That is a hypothesis about one line of strap_asm.py, offered
!     with its own refutation test, and it is for the caller to
!     check, not for this deck to assert.
!
! (B) LINE 32, "C1111(BU,3),,9"         -IF SO,MAKE BCD H
!     printed   000000.00 80  003004.76 70
!     assembled 000000.00 80  003000.36 70     <-- offset 9 lost
!
!     The two commas with nothing between them are really printed:
!     rendered at 1200 dpi, "C1111(BU,3),,9".  Measured, varying
!     only the empty middle field:
!
!       C1111(BU,3),,9     ->  003000.36 70   (offset silently 0)
!       C1111(BU,3),0,9    ->  003004.76 70   (the printed word)
!       C1111(BU,3),0.0,9  ->  003004.76 70   (the printed word)
!       C1111(BU,3), ,9    ->  003000.36 70   (a blank is not enough)
!       C1111(BU,3),,11    ->  003000.36 70   (the offset is simply
!                                              dropped, not misread)
!
!     So an EMPTY address field makes the assembler lose the OFFSET
!     that follows it, SILENTLY -- no error, just a wrong word.
!     That the offset really is 9 is arithmetic, not opinion: the
!     19-bit field is length*1024 + offset, printed as its top 18
!     bits followed by its low bit as 0o40 of the 6-bit group.
!     0o003004 with 0o40 set in the ".76" is 1540*2+1 = 3081 =
!     3*1024 + 9, and 3 is the written (BU,3).  The assembled
!     0o003000 with 0o40 clear in the ".36" is 3072 = 3*1024 + 0.
!     Four other rows on this page confirm the same packing:
!     line 30 (len 1, offset 6) prints 001003, line 37 (len 2,
!     offset 1) prints 002000.52, line 39 (len 12, offset 0)
!     prints 014000, line 41 (len 22, offset 2) prints 026001.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The eleven unit-area field offsets are carried by LANDED decks
! at these same values and this page's printed bits agree with
! every one of them to the bit, which is eleven independent joins
! between this page and pp.99/101/102.
SSETUP  SYN(BU,1),  0.31     ! p.99  line 25 -PSEUDO-OP SET-UP; line 1 prints 000000.37
SCTL    SYN(BU,1),  0.60     ! p.99  line 48 -CONTROL - OPERATION; line 47 prints 000000.74
SFP     SYN(BU,1),  .26      ! p.99  -FILE PROTECT; line 13 prints 000000.32
SFMODE  SYN(BU,2),  2.48     ! p.102 line 13 -FILE MODE; line 23 prints 000002.60
SFDEN   SYN(BU,2),  2.27     ! p.102 line 12 -IOD DENSITY; line 30 prints 000002.33
SIOINS  SYN(BU,64), 4.0      ! p.101 line 17 -IO INSTR. LAST; lines 4/17/44 print 000004.01
SRETAD  SYN(BU,19), 5.0      ! p.101 line 18 -RETURN ADDRESS IN MCP; line 19 prints 000005.00
SSCRPM  SYN(BU,64), 6.0      ! p.101 line 20 -SCRATCH PAD MEMORY; line 39 prints 000006.36
SLABM   SYN(BU,2),  7.16     ! p.101 line 22 -MODE SPECIFIED IN; line 37 prints 000007.20
SLABD   SYN(BU,1),  7.29     ! p.101 line 24 -DENSITY SPECIFIED IN; line 38 prints 000007.35
SCREEL  SYN(BU,18), 8.32     ! p.101 line 28 -CURRENT REEL ADDRESS; line 12 prints 000010.62
! THE TWO NAMED INDEX REGISTERS.  Values measured off this page's
! own bytes (8B on line 48 against lines 1/15, 8D on line 23); the
! SPELLINGS are ink only.  See above.
! >>> NOT THE PRINTED FORM. <<<  p.154 prints "EU SYN,11.0" and
! "EF SYN,13.0"; written that way the index field comes out 0 in
! this assembler.  See the EU/EF section above for the
! measurement.  The VALUES are p.154's own.
EU      SYN, 11              ! p.154 "EU SYN,11.0 -UNIT STATUS TABLE INDEX"; line 48's byte 8B
EF      SYN, 13              ! p.154 "EF SYN,13.0 -FILE AREA TABLE INDEX"; line 23's byte 8D
! The six code externals.  Each printed address below is read off
! THIS page's BINARY OUTPUT column; the NAME and the fact that a
! card defines that address are from elsewhere, as cited.  A SYN
! written ".0" asserts a bit offset this page cannot see -- only
! the half-word bit shows -- so each is written .0 deliberately.
! K SUPP   216242.00  already SYN'd at (8)216242.0 by landed decks
!                     citing printed p.128's defining card
!                     "K SUPP NOP,K COMM R".  Lines 6, 11 and 46
!                     print 216242.03 01, the same LVI three times.
KSUPP   SYN, (8)216242.0
! M IO REJ 221261.00  already SYN'd at (8)221261.0 by landed
!                     decks; lines 7 and 20 print 221261.10 00.
MIOREJ  SYN, (8)221261.0
! K ILEGL  220064.00  decks/mcp_bootstrap_p127.strap SYNs KILEGL
!                     at (8)220064.0 citing printed p.160, where
!                     the label card is printed.
KILEGL  SYN, (8)220064.0
! K K IND  221371.00  NEW TO THIS CORPUS.  The address is line
!                     21's own SIC, which prints a bare 24-bit
!                     address (221371.00 80) and therefore pins it
!                     TO THE BIT, not just to the half word.  The
!                     NAME is read off this page's ink AND
!                     corroborated by cards on TWO OTHER printed
!                     pages, which the volume OCR indexes:
!                     "22C147.4C 221371.CC 80 SIC,K K IND" and
!                     "22C162.40 221371-CC 80 SICK K ING".  This
!                     page's own OCR lines are NOT counted.
KKIND   SYN, (8)221371.0
! K IO IND 221370.00  NEW TO THIS CORPUS.  Line 22's B prints
!                     221370.10 00, so the half word is checked
!                     and the bit within it is not.  One half word
!                     BELOW K K IND, which is the ordinary shape
!                     of a SIC-here/branch-there pair.  Cards on
!                     THREE OTHER printed pages corroborate both
!                     the address and the name, via the volume
!                     OCR: "22C15C.CO 221370.1C CO B K IC INE",
!                     "22C0163.CC 221370.1C CO B,K IO IND" and
!                     "217757.0C 221370.1C O00 B[,]K IC IND
!                     -GO TO IC ING RCUTINE".
KIOIND  SYN, (8)221370.0
! R V4     221201.00  NEW TO THIS CORPUS.  Line 35's LVE prints
!                     221201.01 B0; the .01 is the operation's own
!                     bits, so only the half word is observable.
!                     THE DEFINING CARD IS ON ANOTHER PRINTED PAGE
!                     and the volume OCR reads it cleanly:
!                     "221201.00 COCCO06.CO 8C 000000.20 50  RV4
!                     L(BL),S SCR PM($12)  -BRING LABEL WORD INTC
!                     $R" -- an L at 221201.00 named RV4, which is
!                     exactly what this page branches a value to.
!                     Two further pages print "LVE,9,RV4" against
!                     221201.23 80 and one prints "LVE,O,RV4"
!                     against 221201.C1 BO, byte-for-byte line
!                     35's word.
RV4     SYN, (8)221201.0
! E LAB CW 217765.00  NEW TO THIS CORPUS, and pinned TWICE on this
!                     page: line 36's SV prints 217765.01 30 and
!                     line 45's W prints 217765.03 00.  Both leave
!                     the bit within the half word unobservable.
!                     THE DEFINING CARD IS ON PRINTED p.156 and
!                     the volume OCR of that OTHER page reads it:
!                     "26**217765.00  CCOCCE.00+ ... 217765
!                     E LAB CW  XWS SCR PM($12),1,$  = WRITE LABEL
!                     CTL WO[RD]" -- an XW at 217765.00 named
!                     E LAB CW, which is both the spelling and the
!                     address this page's two instructions demand.
ELABCW  SYN, (8)217765.0

! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ORIGIN.  The page prints NO SLC; this is the heading row's
! 217704 plus line 1's own .40.  See THE JOIN.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)217704.40

! ---- the tail of CHANGE DENSITY ---------------------------------
        CM1111(BU,1),SSETUP($11)     ! line  1
        CNOP                         ! line  2
        LX,0,$+1.                    ! line  3
        SX,0,SIOINS($12)             ! line  4
KDENOP  CTL,0.0($14),0.0             ! line  5  -CHANGE DENSITY COMMAND
        LVI,1,KSUPP                  ! line  6
        B,MIOREJ                     ! line  7  -CHECK IF ACCEPTED
! ---- ***************** WRITE LABEL ***************** (8-10) -----
KWLABL  LVI,1,KSUPP                  ! line 11  -BACKSPACE BLOCK
        LV,9,SCREEL($12)             ! line 12
        BB,SFP($9),KILEGL            ! line 13  -IF FILE PROTECTED, FAKE EPGK
        CNOP                         ! line 14  EMITS NOTHING -- see THE CNOP PARITY CHAIN
        CM1111(BU,1),SSETUP($11)     ! line 15
        LX,0,$+1.                    ! line 16
        SX,0,SIOINS($12)             ! line 17
        CTL,0.0($14),(8)176.         ! line 18  -BSP COMMAND
        SIC,SRETAD($12)              ! line 19
        B,MIOREJ                     ! line 20
        SIC,KKIND                    ! line 21
        B,KIOIND                     ! line 22
        L,SFMODE(EF),1               ! line 23  -LOAD FILE MODE IN $R
        BRZ,ZWLABL                   ! line 24
        CTI0110(BU,2),(8)600000.,1   ! line 25  -TEST IF 11  (NO ECC ODD)
        BZRZ,$+2.0                   ! line 26  -IF NOT,BRANCH
ZWLABL  LFI(BU,6),(8)460000.0        ! line 27  -GET BCD 0 FOR NECC ODD
        B,$+1.32                     ! line 28
        CI0111(BU,6),(8)610000.      ! line 29  -MAKE BCD C OR E
        C0011(BU,1),SFDEN($13),6     ! line 30
        BZRZ,$+2.0                   ! line 31  -TEST IF DESIRED DESITY IS HIGH
        C1111(BU,3),,9               ! line 32  -IF SO,MAKE BCD H
        B,$+1.32                     ! line 33
        CI0111(BU,6),(8)420000.,6    ! line 34  -IF NOT,MAKE BCD L FOR LOW DENS
        LVE,0,RV4                    ! line 35
        SV,0,ELABCW                  ! line 36
        SF(BU,2),SLABM($12),1        ! line 37  -SET MODE
        SF(BU,1),SLABD($12),6        ! line 38  -SET DENSITY
        SF(BU,12),SSCRPM+.30($12)    ! line 39  -SET UP LABEL
        LI(BU,24),(8)202020.20       ! line 40
        SF(BU,22),SSCRPM.42($12),2   ! line 41
        CNOP                         ! line 42
        LX,0,$+1.0                   ! line 43
        SX,0,SIOINS($12)             ! line 44
        W,0.0($14),ELABCW            ! line 45  -WRITE LABEL COMMAND
        LVI,1,KSUPP                  ! line 46
        CM0000(BU,1),SCTL($11)       ! line 47
        CM1111,SSETUP(EU)            ! line 48  -TURN ON SET UP BIT

